
%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:capsicum@

%9Parameters:%9

    ENTER:       Enters capability mode. There is no way back: erssi stays
                 in it until it exits.
    STATUS:      Says whether erssi is in capability mode.

    Without an argument the subcommands are listed.

%9Description:%9

    Capsicum is the capability and sandbox framework of FreeBSD. In
    capability mode erssi can no longer reach the file system or the
    network by itself, which limits what a security hole in erssi or in a
    library could do. Only an erssi built with Capsicum support has this
    command and its settings: the default on FreeBSD (meson option
    with-capsicum).

    When the mode is entered, erssi sets up TLS, creates the directory
    capsicum_irclogs_path if it is missing and starts a small helper
    process outside the sandbox, which makes the connections to servers
    and the name lookups for it. If one of these steps fails, erssi stays
    as it was and says why ("Capability mode failed").

    In capability mode:

        - files can be opened only below capsicum_irclogs_path, the
          directory it named when the mode was entered (a later change
          takes effect after a restart). That covers the log files
          (autolog, /LOG, /RAWLOG, the Mentions log) and /CAT; every
          other file erssi would read or write from then on (the config,
          scripts, themes) cannot be opened,
        - /SAVE and the automatic save of the settings cannot write the
          config,
        - /SEARCH is not available,
        - servers can be reached only on the ports from
          capsicum_port_min to capsicum_port_max,
        - erssi waits while the helper looks up a server name: with a
          slow or broken DNS server it does not react (nor answer the
          pings of its other connections) until the lookup ends. A
          server given by its IP address needs no lookup.

    The default config writes autolog files to ~/.erssi/logs and the
    Mentions log to ~/.erssi/logs/mentions.log. With the default
    capsicum_irclogs_path (~/irclogs) they stop being written in
    capability mode: set capsicum_irclogs_path to ~/.erssi/logs first.
    awaylog_file (~/.erssi/away.log by default) has to be below it too.

    Entering the mode at every start (/SET capsicum ON, then /SAVE) is
    better than /CAPSICUM ENTER by hand, which leaves the connections
    made before it outside the sandbox. docs/capsicum.txt in the erssi
    sources has more.

%9Settings:%9

    capsicum (boolean; default: OFF)
        Enters capability mode when erssi has started.
    capsicum_irclogs_path (default: ~/irclogs)
        The only directory erssi can open files in while in capability
        mode; created when the mode is entered.
    capsicum_port_min, capsicum_port_max (default: 6667 and 9999)
        The server ports that can be connected to in capability mode.

%9Examples:%9

    /SET capsicum_irclogs_path ~/.erssi/logs
    /SET awaylog_file ~/.erssi/logs/away.log
    /SET capsicum ON
    /SAVE
    /CAPSICUM STATUS

%9See also:%9 CAT, LOG, SAVE, SEARCH

